Kansas"]},{"id":"scholarly","score":0.48,"confidence":"medium","evidence":["catalog text contains \"historians\"","academic press signal: University Press of Kansas / University Press of Kansas"]},{"id":"narrative","score":0.34,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"story of\"","catalog text contains \"murder\""]},{"id":"character_driven","score":0.22,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"life of\""]},{"id":"synthesis","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"from\"","catalog text contains \"history of\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Putnam County, Indiana","confidence":0.88}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"The Civil War transformed Putnam County’s everyday life by temporarily displacing economic and temperance concerns and shifting northern whites’ racial attitudes, with pensions enabling some women’s independence.","confidence":0.62},"academicOrientation":{"score":65,"confidence":0.55},"profileConfidence":0.78,"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"392e4ffb854e77bc4ae536ad4adea2e490b013559e4060d1407409ebb77b94b2","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"summary":"For all that has been written about the Civil War's impact on the urban northeast and southern home fronts, we have until now lacked a detailed picture of how it affected specific communities in the Union's Midwestern heartland. In this book the author offers a deeply researched micro history of one such community, Putnam County, Indiana, from the Compromise of 1850 to the end of Reconstruction, and shows how its citizens responded to and were affected by the war. Delving into the everyday life of a small town in one of the nineteenth century's bellwether states, this work considers the Civil War within a much broader chronological context than other accounts. It ranges across three decades to show how the issues of the day, particularly race and sectionalism, temporarily displaced economic and temperance concerns, how the racial attitudes of northern whites changed, and how a generation of young men and women coped with the transformative experience of war. The author interrelates a wide range of topics. Through temperance and alcohol she illustrates nativism and class consciousness, while through an account of a murder she probes ethnicity, politics, and gender. She reveals how some women wanted to maintain dependence and how the war gave independence to others, as pensions allowed them to survive without a male provider.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Social aspects","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-generation-at-war-the-civil-war-era-in-a-northern-community-nicole-etcheson"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Race relations","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-generation-at-war-the-civil-war-era-in-a-northern-community-nicole-etcheson"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"History","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-generation-at-war-the-civil-war-era-in-a-northern-community-nicole-etcheson"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Indiana, history","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-generation-at-war-the-civil-war-era-in-a-northern-community-nicole-etcheson"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"United states, history, civil war, 1861-1865","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-generation-at-war-the-civil-war-era-in-a-northern-community-nicole-etcheson"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"United states, race relations","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-generation-at-war-the-civil-war-era-in-a-northern-community-nicole-etcheson"}],"displaySummary":"For all that has been written about the Civil War's impact on the urban northeast and southern home fronts, we have until now lacked a detailed picture of how it affected specific communities in the Union's Midwestern heartland. In this book the author offers a deeply researched micro history of one such community, Putnam County, Indiana, from the Compromise of 1850 to the end of Reconstruction, and shows how its citizens responded to and were affected by the war. Delving into the everyday life of a small town in one of the nineteenth century's bellwether states, this work considers the Civil War within a much broader chronological context than other accounts. It ranges across three decades to show how the issues of the day, particularly race and sectionalism, temporarily displaced economic and temperance concerns, how the racial attitudes of northern whites changed, and how a generation of young men and women coped with the transformative experience of war. The author interrelates a wide range of topics. Through temperance and alcohol she illustrates nativism and class consciousness, while through an account of a murder she probes ethnicity, politics, and gender. She reveals how some women wanted to maintain dependence and how the war gave independence to others, as pensions allowed them to survive without a male provider.","primarySubject":"Race & Ethnicity","primaryTopic":"American Civil War","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Race & Ethnicity","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Race & Ethnicity","score":12,"evidenceCount":2},{"subject":"History","score":9,"evidenceCount":3},{"subject":"War & Military","score":6,"evidenceCount":1},{"subject":"American History","score":5,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"Race relations","mappedSubject":"Race & Ethnicity","score":6,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-generation-at-war-the-civil-war-era-in-a-northern-community-nicole-etcheson","id":"subject-evidence-boo